---
id: fragile-presentation
type: definition
name: Fragile Presentation
category: fragile-presentation
targets: [source]
related: [unsafe-rendering]
tensions: []
rules: [FRAG-001]
---

## What It Is

Authoring content invisible and depending on a runtime animation to make
it visible. The construction is an element whose initial style is
`opacity: 0` paired with an entrance animation that is supposed to bring
it to `opacity: 1` — in JSX, the motion/framer-motion idiom
`initial={{ opacity: 0 }}` with `animate` or `whileInView`. The content's
presence on screen is gated on JavaScript executing and the animation
trigger firing.

## Why It's Harmful

- **The failure is silent and total.** If the trigger never fires, the
  section renders permanently blank — no error, no console noise, no
  fallback. The page looks finished and is missing its content.
- **Triggers fail in real environments.** A `prefers-reduced-motion`
  setting can skip the animation while the initial style stays applied; a
  backgrounded tab may never deliver the viewport/scroll trigger for a
  below-fold reveal; a hydration error strands server-rendered markup at
  its initial style; blocked or failed JavaScript leaves the element at
  `opacity: 0` forever.
- **It survives review.** On the author's machine the animation always
  fires, so the trap is invisible in development and code review alike.
- **Agents apply entrance animations by default.** AI-generated marketing
  and dashboard code reaches for a scroll-reveal on every section, so the
  construction ships at scale precisely in the code Anvil governs.

## The Spectrum

- **Warn (severity warning, on by default):** a single-line
  `initial={{ ... opacity: 0 ... }}` (FRAG-001). It warns rather than
  blocks because a mount-triggered `animate` on above-the-fold content
  with a reduced-motion fallback usually reaches its end state; the
  scroll-gated `whileInView` variant on body content is the harmful end,
  and a line regex cannot tell the two apart — the rule surfaces the
  construction so a human confirms the fallback contract (the ADR-087
  construction-smell posture).
- **Benign (intentionally not flagged):** entrances that start visible —
  transform-only initials (`initial={{ y: 20 }}`), partial opacity
  (`opacity: 0.4`), `initial={false}`, exit fades
  (`exit={{ opacity: 0 }}`), and CSS hover/focus transitions. Losing the
  motion must cost the polish, never the content.
- **Known false-positive surface:** the scanner is line-regex based and
  does not strip comments or string bodies; a purely decorative element
  animated from `opacity: 0` is also a judgement call. Suppress with a
  reason naming the fallback (or the decorative role).

## The Right Response

1. **Content visible by default** — never gate presence on an animation
   completing.
2. **Animate decoration, not existence** — enter from a visible state
   (`initial={{ y: 20 }}` → `whileInView={{ y: 0 }}`); a missed trigger
   then costs the motion, not the content.
3. **A deliberate fade-in must guarantee its end state** — respect
   `prefers-reduced-motion` (`useReducedMotion` /
   `<MotionConfig reducedMotion="user">`), and give a scroll-gated reveal
   a mount-time or timeout fallback so `opacity: 1` is reached even when
   the viewport trigger never fires.
4. If a visible reduced-motion/no-JS fallback provably exists — or the
   element is pure decoration — suppress FRAG-001 with a reason naming
   it.

## Detection Signals

- **Lexical:** the single-line JSX prop
  `initial={{ ... opacity: 0 ... }}` (FRAG-001). The zero must terminate
  (`,` or `}` follows, whitespace allowed) so `opacity: 0.4` does not
  trip.
- **Allowlisted contexts:** test files (`*.test.*`, `*.spec.*`,
  `__tests__/`, `tests/`) and `explain/` fixtures opt out — they
  demonstrate the construction intentionally.
- **Scope:** JavaScript/TypeScript extensions only (`.ts`, `.tsx`, `.js`,
  `.jsx`, `.mjs`, `.cjs`) — the idiom is a JSX prop; `.html`/`.css` are
  retired from the scan set.
- **Negative signals (do not fire):** `initial={{ opacity: 1 }}`,
  transform-only initials, `exit={{ opacity: 0 }}`, partial opacity
  values.
- **Out of detection (known gaps):** multi-line `initial` objects,
  `variants` objects (`hidden: { opacity: 0 }`), IntersectionObserver +
  class-toggle reveals, and utility-class idioms (`opacity-0`). These
  exceed the line-regex engine and wait on a JS/TS AST detection path
  (ADR-071 precedent) rather than a stretched regex.

## Example

```tsx
// FRAG-001 trips — content exists only if the reveal fires:
<motion.section
  initial={{ opacity: 0, y: 20 }} whileInView={{ opacity: 1, y: 0 }}>
  <PricingTable />
</motion.section>

// Fine — visible by default; the animation is decoration:
<motion.section initial={{ y: 20 }} whileInView={{ y: 0 }}>
  <PricingTable />
</motion.section>
```
